---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "Brand Reviews",
    tags: [
      "muscleblaze",
      "muscle blaze",
      "muscle blaze protein powder",
      "mb protein",
      "muscleblaze biozyme whey"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{
    text: "See Our Top Whey Protein Picks",
    link: "/best-whey-protein-powder/",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker="Brand Review" badge="Label-Checked" author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">MuscleBlaze Protein Review</Fragment>
      MuscleBlaze is one of India's biggest sports nutrition brands, and its Biozyme whey is the product most people mean when they search for MB protein. Here is what is on the labels, which tub to buy, and what to buy instead if you live in the US.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#verdict">The Short Verdict</a>
      <a href="#where-sold">Who Makes It and Where</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">The Range, Ranked</a>
      <a href="#biozyme">Biozyme Label</a>
      <a href="#enzymes">Do the Enzymes Matter?</a>
      <a href="#range">The Rest of the Range</a>
      <a href="#testing">Testing and Fakes</a>
      <a href="#cost">Cost per 25 g</a>
      <a href="#outside-india">Buying Outside India</a>
      <a href="#who-should-skip">Who Should Skip It</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          MuscleBlaze is a solid choice if you live in India and a hard one to justify if you do not. Its Biozyme Performance Whey delivers 25 g of protein per scoop with unusually visible third-party testing for the Indian market, and its Raw Whey line is a good-value plain protein. But it is an Indian brand sold mainly through Indian retailers, the flagship scoop is large for the protein it delivers, and US buyers have easier options on Amazon.com.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Is MuscleBlaze Any Good? The Short Verdict
          </h2>

          <p>
            For buyers in India, yes. MuscleBlaze is one of the most widely sold sports nutrition brands in the country, Amazon India featured it among the brands in its 2026 Protein Week promotion, and its flagship Biozyme Performance Whey lists <strong>25 g of protein per 36 g scoop</strong>. Unusually for the Indian market, the brand puts third-party testing front and center, with retailer listings citing Informed Choice, Labdoor and Trustified Gold testing for the Biozyme line.
          </p>

          <p>
            The weaknesses are worth knowing before you buy. The Biozyme scoop is large: 36 g of powder for 25 g of protein is roughly 69 percent protein by weight, lower than a typical US whey blend, because the tub also carries flavor, cocoa and the enzyme blend. The headline enzyme claim is backed mostly by the brand's own research. And outside India, availability is patchy and largely through third-party importers.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our ranking is simple. Most buyers in India should start with <strong>Biozyme Performance Whey</strong>. If you want a plain, unflavored protein at the lowest cost per gram, buy <strong>Raw Whey Protein Concentrate</strong>. If you are lactose sensitive or cutting, <strong>Whey Gold</strong> is the isolate to choose. Skip the Beginner's Whey, which lists only 12 g of protein per scoop and makes little sense for most adults.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are in the US, the practical answer is different: buy a comparable US whey with a brand-sold Amazon listing, such as Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard, rather than paying import prices for a tub with uncertain storage history. Our <a href="/best-whey-protein-powder/">best whey protein powder</a> roundup ranks those options in detail.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="where-sold" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Who Makes MuscleBlaze and Where It Is Sold
          </h2>

          <p>
            MuscleBlaze is an Indian brand. Its labels name <strong>Bright Lifecare Pvt. Ltd.</strong>, based in Gurugram, Haryana, as the marketer, with manufacturing listed at Bright Lifecare's own facility in Manesar and at Sapiens Labs in Himachal Pradesh. Retailer listings for Biozyme also say the whey is sourced from the USA and Europe, which is common for Indian brands because large-scale whey production is concentrated in those regions.
          </p>

          <p>
            Inside India it is easy to find. MuscleBlaze sells through its own site, Amazon India and major online pharmacies such as Tata 1mg and PharmEasy, in pack sizes from single 36 g sachets up to 4 kg jars. Amazon India's 2026 Protein Week, a promotion with discounts on whey and plant protein, listed MuscleBlaze among its featured homegrown brands alongside international names such as Isopure and Dymatize.
          </p>

          <p>
            Outside India, the picture is far less clear. We could not find MuscleBlaze sold by the brand or a named authorized distributor on Amazon.com at the time of writing. Any MuscleBlaze listing you do find on US marketplaces is most likely a third-party import, which raises questions about storage, freshness and whether the tub carries the right labeling for the US market. The buy buttons on the MuscleBlaze cards below run a tagged Amazon search, so check the seller carefully before you order.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is why this review includes two US alternatives. For an Indian buyer, MuscleBlaze competes directly with Optimum Nutrition, Dymatize, Isopure and Myprotein, all sold in India at import prices. For a US buyer, those international brands are the local, cheaper option. If you are weighing Myprotein as well, our <a href="/myprotein-review/">Myprotein review</a> covers how its range compares across markets.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The MuscleBlaze Range, Ranked
          </h2>

          <p>
            Four MuscleBlaze powders cover what most buyers need, followed by two alternatives that are easy to buy on Amazon.com for readers in the US. MuscleBlaze label figures come from Indian retailer listings at the time of writing and vary slightly by flavor and pack size, so check the panel on your tub.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="biozyme" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Biozyme Performance Whey: The Label in Detail
          </h2>

          <p>
            Per Indian retailer listings at the time of writing, one level 36 g scoop of Biozyme Performance Whey provides <strong>25 g of protein, 11.75 g of essential amino acids and 5.51 g of branched-chain amino acids</strong>. The protein blend is listed at 91 percent of the formula and is made from whey protein concentrate and whey powder. It is sweetened with sucralose and carries no added sugar, and the listings describe it as vegetarian.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Biozyme Performance Whey, per 36 g scoop (listing)</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">25 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Protein</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">11.75 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Essential amino acids</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">5.51 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">BCAAs</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">36 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Scoop size</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The scoop size is the number to watch. A typical US whey blend delivers 24 to 25 g of protein in a 30 to 32 g scoop. Biozyme needs 36 g for 25 g, so a 1 kg jar holds about 27 to 28 servings rather than the 31 to 33 you might expect from a leaner formula. That is not a sign of a bad product, but it does mean you should compare cost per 25 g of protein rather than price per kilogram.
          </p>

          <p>
            The amino acid figures are reasonable for whey. Around 5.5 g of BCAAs per 25 g of protein is in line with what dairy protein naturally contains, which suggests the protein figure is coming from whey rather than added free amino acids. That matters in India in particular, where label accuracy has been a recurring concern and independent testing programs grew up specifically to catch amino spiking.
          </p>

          <p>
            Biozyme also comes in a variant called Biozyme Whey PR, which retailer listings show at 30 g of protein per serving with added creatine monohydrate. It is a reasonable choice for someone who wants both in one scoop, but most people get more flexibility from buying creatine separately, because the useful daily dose of creatine is small and fixed while protein needs vary.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="enzymes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Do the Biozyme Enzymes Actually Matter?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The selling point of the Biozyme line is <strong>MB EnzymePro</strong>, a proteolytic enzyme blend that the brand says improves protein digestion and absorption. Retailer listings describe the formula as clinically studied and point to a trial registered with the Clinical Trials Registry of India, run under the Indian Council of Medical Research. The brand also cites a 2021 NutraIngredients award for the product.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is more evidence than many proprietary blends have, but it is worth reading carefully. The research is brand-linked, and improved absorption in a study does not automatically translate into more muscle. Healthy adults already digest and absorb whey protein very efficiently, so the room for improvement is small. Where added enzymes may help is in comfort: some people report less heaviness or bloating with enzyme-added powders, although evidence for that is limited and individual.
          </p>

          <p>
            The bigger drivers of results are well established: total daily protein, spreading it across meals, and training. A scoop with 25 g of whey protein and over 2 g of leucine does the job regardless of the enzyme blend. If you are unsure how much you need, our <a href="/how-much-protein-do-i-need/">how much protein do I need</a> guide walks through the numbers for different goals.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our view is that the enzymes are a small potential bonus, not a reason on their own to pay more. If Biozyme is priced close to a plain whey you trust, the enzymes are a fine tiebreaker. If it costs noticeably more per 25 g of protein than the Raw Whey line, the plain concentrate is the better buy for most people.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="range" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Whey Gold, Raw Whey and the Rest of the Range
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Whey Gold</strong> is MuscleBlaze's isolate. Retailer listings describe it as 100 percent whey protein isolate and show about 30 g of protein per 36.5 g serving, sweetened with sucralose and independently tested by Labdoor for label accuracy and purity. As an isolate, it carries less lactose, fat and carbohydrate than Biozyme, which makes it the better pick for cutting or for people who get bloated on concentrate. Our <a href="/whey-vs-whey-isolate/">whey vs whey isolate</a> guide explains the difference.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Raw Whey Protein Concentrate</strong> is the plain, unflavored option, listed at 24 g of protein per scoop with added digestive enzymes and described as 80 percent concentrate. There is no sweetener or flavoring to work around, so it disappears into smoothies, oats and cooking. It is usually the lowest cost per 25 g of protein in the range, which makes it the value pick for anyone who does not need a flavored shake.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Raw Whey Isolate</strong> is the unflavored isolate, listed at 27 g of protein per scoop and about 16 servings in a 500 g pack, with added digestive enzymes. It costs more than the concentrate per gram of protein but suits people who want the cleanest possible label and lower lactose. If unflavored protein appeals to you in general, see our <a href="/best-unflavored-protein-powder/">best unflavored protein powder</a> picks.
          </p>

          <p>
            The product to avoid for most adults is <strong>Beginner's Whey</strong>, which retailer listings show at just 12 g of protein per scoop. The brand positions it as an entry product, but at half the protein of a standard scoop you pay for a lot of non-protein powder. Two scoops of a beginner product rarely beat one scoop of Biozyme or Raw Whey on cost or nutrition.
          </p>

