        <div id="outside-india" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Buying MuscleBlaze Outside India
          </h2>

          <p>
            If you live in the US and have seen MuscleBlaze recommended by Indian fitness creators, the honest advice is to buy a comparable US product instead. You will pay less, get a fresher tub with US labeling, and have a clear return path. The closest equivalents are a mainstream whey blend for Biozyme, an isolate for Whey Gold, and an unflavored whey for Raw Whey.
          </p>

          <p>
            For Biozyme, the obvious alternative is <strong>Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard 100% Whey</strong>, which delivers about 24 g of protein and roughly 120 calories per scoop per the label and is widely available on Amazon.com. For Raw Whey, <strong>Naked Whey</strong> is a single-ingredient grass-fed whey concentrate with about 25 g of protein per serving per the label. Both appear as alternative cards in the picks above.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you still want MuscleBlaze specifically, check three things before you buy. Confirm who the seller is and where the stock ships from, check the manufacturing and best-before dates in the listing photos or by asking the seller, and make sure the label is intact and matches the batch-report format the brand uses. If any of that is unclear, do not buy.
          </p>

          <p>
            For readers in other markets, such as the Gulf states or Southeast Asia, availability varies by country and changes often. The same rules apply: prefer the brand's own channels or a large authorized retailer, and compare cost per 25 g of protein against international brands sold locally. Our <a href="/amazon-protein-powder/">guide to buying protein powder on Amazon</a> covers how to spot risky third-party listings.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Who Should Skip MuscleBlaze
          </h2>

          <p>
            Skip it if you <strong>live outside India</strong>. The value case depends on local pricing and distribution, and abroad you will usually pay more for a tub with a less certain supply chain. A US or European whey with comparable testing is the more sensible buy.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip Biozyme if you are <strong>lactose sensitive</strong>. It is a concentrate-based formula, and the enzyme blend is aimed at protein digestion rather than lactose. Whey Gold or Raw Whey Isolate are the better options in the range, and anyone with more than mild symptoms should speak to a doctor.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip it if you are <strong>dairy-free or vegan</strong>. The products reviewed here are all whey-based. A plant protein from a brand with published heavy-metal testing is the right category for you instead.
          </p>

          <p>
            And as with any supplement, talk to a doctor or registered dietitian before using protein powder if you have kidney disease, are pregnant or breastfeeding, take regular medication, or are buying for a teenager. This review is based on label analysis and published listings, not hands-on testing, and it is not medical advice. If muscle gain is your main goal, our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-muscle-gain/">best protein powder for muscle gain</a> guide covers the wider field.
          </p>
