          </h2>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is MuscleBlaze a good protein brand?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  For buyers in India, yes. Its Biozyme Performance Whey lists 25 g of protein per 36 g scoop, retailer listings cite testing by Informed Choice, Labdoor and Trustified, and the brand publishes batch lab reports. Its weaknesses are a large scoop for the protein delivered, enzyme claims based mostly on brand-linked research, and limited availability outside India.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is MuscleBlaze available on Amazon US?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  We could not find MuscleBlaze sold by the brand or a named authorized distributor on Amazon.com at the time of writing. It is sold widely on Amazon India and Indian retailers. Any listing you see on US marketplaces is most likely a third-party import, so check the seller, the dates and the label carefully, or buy a comparable US whey instead.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>How much protein is in MuscleBlaze Biozyme?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Biozyme Performance Whey lists 25 g of protein per 36 g scoop, with 11.75 g of essential amino acids and 5.51 g of BCAAs, per Indian retailer listings at the time of writing. A related product, Biozyme Whey PR, lists 30 g of protein per serving with added creatine. Figures vary slightly by flavor, so check your tub.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Who owns MuscleBlaze?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  MuscleBlaze labels name Bright Lifecare Pvt. Ltd., based in Gurugram, Haryana, as the marketer. Manufacturing is listed at Bright Lifecare's facility in Manesar and at Sapiens Labs in Himachal Pradesh. Retailer listings say the whey itself is sourced from the USA and Europe, which is typical for Indian protein brands.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does the Biozyme enzyme blend work?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  MuscleBlaze says its MB EnzymePro blend improves protein absorption and cites a trial registered with India's clinical trials registry. The research is brand-linked, and healthy adults already absorb whey very efficiently, so the real-world benefit is probably small. Some people report better digestive comfort with enzyme-added powders, but total daily protein matters far more.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Which MuscleBlaze protein is best for beginners?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Biozyme Performance Whey is the most sensible starting point despite the name of the Beginner's Whey product. Beginner's Whey lists only 12 g of protein per scoop, so it delivers much less protein per rupee. If you prefer an unflavored option for smoothies, Raw Whey Protein Concentrate is the cheapest per 25 g of protein in the range.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is MuscleBlaze better than Optimum Nutrition?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  In India, MuscleBlaze is often cheaper per 25 g of protein and has strong local testing claims, while Optimum Nutrition is an imported product priced accordingly. In the US the situation reverses: Gold Standard is easy to buy fresh on Amazon at a similar or lower cost per 25 g. Choose based on where you live, not on the brand name.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

